The shortcut types also play a role. Trial 1 uses identity shortcuts, which are simple and effective for shallower networks. Trial 7 uses projection shortcuts when the dimensions change, which helps in maintaining the flow of gradients through the network without causing dimension mismatches. The plain-34 doesn't use any shortcuts, leading to higher error.

So, putting this together: Deeper networks with bottleneck blocks and appropriate shortcut connections perform better on ImageNet. ResNet-101 seems to be the top performer here, which aligns with what I know about deeper ResNets being state-of-the-art for a while.

